00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:08.790 align:start position:0%
 
this morning on the dish  a passion for pasta and all the food of  his native italy  frank di pasquale is one of the most  successful tours in boston's  north end the city's little italy along  with seven dining venues he has dozens  of other ventures  including a pasta shop a bakery even a  hotel  and a magazine he didn't start out in  the field but  soon found food was his first love and  it all began  with a simple sandwich this is our love  this is our passion  this is what we live for it's just the  love of what we do  with a veritable feast before him boston  restaurateur frank di pasquale  speaks from the heart i wake up i can't  wait  to see a new dish that the chef is  making i can't wait to see the smile on  the customer's face as they're sipping  their wine or eating the food or  saying something nice on the way out  this array of  favorite deposqually ventures dishes  includes zucchini pasta with pravolla  parmigiano and reggiano  margarita pizza with hand crushed  tomatoes gnocchiala sorrentina  even a signature espresso martini but  dipasquale  sets his sights on the simple sandwich  he says started it all  homemade prosciutto de palma buffalo  mozzarella  tomatoes mine ripe tomatoes extra virgin  olive oil  a little bit of basil my first  restaurant is named under that  particular sandwich and we started it as  a panino and we turned it into a  trattoria  which happened in 1987 which is i think  about 34 years ago longer than we wanted  and here we are today with seven  different restaurants  dipasquale's father was the first to  dream of building a food business here  in the united states  bringing his family to boston from san  giuseppe italy  the city's north end quickly became home  this is honestly the greatest  neighborhood probably in the country  it's because people make places places  don't make people  all right guys and outside of every door  you will see  either an owner or a manager greeting  you loving you  hugging you my father was here all the  time making sure that naples won the  soccer match of the week  and we had espressos here and he would  buy me a little taroni  and or confetti and i was always by his  side  from when i was four years old till the  the day he passed away  just 15 at the time of his father's  death depasquale says  that's when he became the man of the  family after college he launched his  first business  a shoe store i didn't like the fact that  i had to sit there all day long and  and size people's feet and so so i said  you know something my  my real passion is food so let me get  started with food that's when that first  north end restaurant il penino came to  be this  started off as a simple sandwich shop  deep esquali traces his culinary  yearnings  to his trips back to italy most of my  friends they'd be looking at the  beautiful girls at the beach  but i spent most of my time in kitchens  in restaurants because  that was my passion to watch the pizza  man the way he slapped the pizza how he  turned it in the pizza oven  how he crushed the tomatoes by hand i  watched the people make the zucchini  pasta the mothers  make the gnocchi it was a passion it was  lovely everything that i always wanted  to be  it's those italian traditions that de  pisquale has brought to each of his  restaurant ventures  like homemade buffalo mozzarella  and fresh baked bread but it's more than  just the food  i always wanted to be the best and i  always say it in all my meetings  people don't remember who comes in  second so my  stride was to be the very best  restaurant that i could be and i hired  the right people to do the right job  you brought chefs over from italy i  brought chefs over from italy and a lot  of them  ended up staying here a lot of them  ended up marrying here and a lot of them  ended up never leaving again and they're  still a part of my team  a team that has a huge footprint in that  same north end dipasquale first  wandered in as a child and in some ways  not much has changed we start every  morning at my coffee shop we have our  espresso  cappuccino and we talk about last night  we talk about our food  like i said before this is the greatest  community in the country we help each  other with menus we help each other  with buying we if someone runs out of a  certain product they could definitely  come to my restaurant  or i could go to their restaurant frank  that seems so  rare to me because you are competitors  and you're right next door that you  would help each other  in that way but competitors  bring up our game so so we all become a  little bit better  that community bond never more important  than during the pandemic  with the restaurant industry one of the  hardest hit  well it's a different world and we're  adjusting to it we're buying  umbrellas together we're buying outdoor  furniture together to save a few dollars  we're buying barriers we're teaching  each other the distancing  and it's been a tough time but honestly  we begged  to have outside seating for as long as  i've been here and that's  at least 34 years and it took a pandemic  to get us here  but you have it now the unofficial mayor  of the north end  deposquali would be the one to find the  positive spin  he's also the one that even in tough  times is trying to look ahead  his next project a joint venture in  boston's new encore casino hotel  we're going to have a pizzeria very  similar to the one that i have at  quattro  how old are you oh do i have to say you  don't if you if you don't want to  68. do you ever going on 55.  i was going to say do you ever see not  working at this pace  i think i i i think with the espresso  martinis and  and um and the espressos that i have  every single day which is usually about  five or six of them  um it keeps me going and i i think i'm  gonna hit  uh i'm gonna try to hit that hundred  someday also can i say cheers to that  cheers to that and as we toast that  future  dipasquale can't help but turn to the  past in reflecting upon  all of his success if you could have  this meal  with anyone past or present who would it  be oh wow  wow i think i think it would be with my  father  i think my father joseph i mean he had  the vision to come to america and give  us this opportunity  and i'm not going to waste it he hasn't  you taste that food there's love in that  food i blew it  i was reminded by brian applegate our  executive producer  espresso martinis it would have been so  easy to bring to you so  espresso martinis i thought you were  already bringing them  you were fair yeah i did i did yeah  post show and some buffalo mozzarella  too buffalo  good it's all good

